# sqlc .NET plugin

A [sqlc](https://sqlc.dev) plugin that generates a **C# / .NET data-access
library** from your PostgreSQL schema and SQL queries.

You write plain SQL; the plugin gives you a ready-to-build .NET project with
strongly typed, async methods for every query, result records, a
dependency-injection extension and transaction support — built on
[Npgsql](https://www.npgsql.org/).

---

## Requirements

| Tool | Version | Used for |
|------|---------|----------|
| [Go](https://go.dev/dl/) | 1.27.3+ | Building the plugin |
| [sqlc](https://docs.sqlc.dev/en/latest/overview/install.html) | with plugin support (config `version: "2"`) | Generating code |
| [.NET SDK](https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download) | matching your `netVersion` (sample: `net11.0`) | Building the generated library |
| [Docker](https://www.docker.com/) | any recent | Local PostgreSQL for the sample (optional) |
| [goose](https://github.com/pressly/goose) | v3 | Running the sample migrations (optional) |
| `make` | — | Convenience targets |

### Dependencies

- **Plugin:** `github.com/sqlc-dev/plugin-sdk-go` v1.23.0
- **Generated .NET project:** `Npgsql` 10.0.3, `Npgsql.DependencyInjection` 10.0.3
- **Your app:** `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection` (to use the generated registration method)

---

## Installation

```bash
make build
```

This produces the plugin binary at `./bin/net-plugin`.

---

## Usage

### 1. Configure sqlc

Add the plugin to your `sqlc.yaml`:

```yaml
version: "2"
plugins:
  - name: net-plugin
    process:
      cmd: /path/to/bin/net-plugin
sql:
  - engine: postgresql
    schema: migrations
    queries: queries/
    codegen:
      - plugin: net-plugin
        out: gen/
        options:
          projName: Project.Data
          netVersion: net11.0
          namespace: Project.Data
          class: Db
          extFunc: AddDb
```

| Option       | Description                                          |
|--------------|------------------------------------------------------|
| `projName`   | Name of the generated project (`<projName>.csproj`)  |
| `netVersion` | Target framework, e.g. `net8.0`, `net11.0`           |
| `namespace`  | Namespace for the generated code                     |
| `class`      | Name of the database class registered in DI          |
| `extFunc`    | Name of the `IServiceCollection` extension method    |

All options are required.

### 2. Write queries

Annotate each query with a name and a command:

```sql
-- name: GetAuthor :one
SELECT * FROM authors WHERE id = @id LIMIT 1;

-- name: ListAuthors :many
SELECT * FROM authors
WHERE sqlc.narg(min_id)::bigint IS NULL OR id > sqlc.narg(min_id);

-- name: DeleteAuthor :exec
DELETE FROM authors WHERE id = $1;
```

| Command | Returns                                         |
|---------|-------------------------------------------------|
| `:one`  | `Task<{Name}Record?>` — `null` if no row found  |
| `:many` | `Task<IEnumerable<{Name}Record>>`               |
| `:exec` | `Task<int>` — number of affected rows           |

Tips:

- Parameters can be written in one of these ways:
  - `$1`, `$2`, … — positional; the argument name is taken from the matching column.
  - `@name` — named parameter.
  - `sqlc.arg(name)` / `sqlc.narg(name)` — named parameter; `narg` makes it optional (nullable).
- Don't mix positional (`$1`) and named (`@name`, `sqlc.arg`/`sqlc.narg`) parameters in the same query.
- Column names become `PascalCase` properties, arguments become `camelCase`.

### 3. Generate

```bash
sqlc generate
```

Reference the generated `.csproj` from your application. Don't edit the
generated files — they are overwritten on every run.

### 4. Use it in .NET

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Project.Data;

var services = new ServiceCollection();
services.AddDb("Host=localhost;Port=5434;Database=sqlc_test;Username=postgres;Password=postgres");

var provider = services.BuildServiceProvider();
var db = provider.GetRequiredService<Db>();

var query = new Query(db);

var author  = await query.GetAuthor(1);
var authors = await query.ListAuthors(minId: null, minCreatedAt: null, sortBy: "created_at");
var deleted = await query.DeleteAuthor(1);
```

#### Transactions

```csharp
await using var tx = await db.BeginTx();
var q = new Query(tx);

await q.UpdateAuthor(id, "new name", "new bio");
await q.CreateBook(id, "Title", "isbn-123");

await tx.Commit();   // or: await tx.Rollback();
```

If the transaction is disposed without `Commit()`, it is rolled back.

### Supported types

| PostgreSQL                                                  | C#         |
|-------------------------------------------------------------|------------|
| `boolean`                                                   | `bool`     |
| `smallint`                                                  | `short`    |
| `integer`, `int4`                                           | `int`      |
| `bigint`, `int8`, `bigserial`                               | `long`     |
| `real`                                                      | `float`    |
| `double precision`                                          | `double`   |
| `numeric`, `money`                                          | `decimal`  |
| `uuid`                                                      | `Guid`     |
| `timestamp`, `timestamptz`, `date`                          | `DateTime` |
| `interval`                                                  | `TimeSpan` |
| `text`, `varchar`, `character`, `citext`, `json`, `jsonb`   | `string`   |

Nullable columns become nullable C# types (`T?`) and arrays become C# arrays
(`text[]` → `string[]`).

---

## Running the sample

The `test/` folder contains a complete example: migrations, queries, and a
console app that uses the generated library.

```
test/
├── sqlc.yaml                          # sqlc config using the plugin
├── migrations/                        # schema (goose migrations)
│   ├── 00001_create_authors.sql
│   ├── 00002_create_books.sql
│   └── 00003_add_tags_to_authors.sql
├── queries/                           # annotated SQL queries
│   ├── authors.sql
│   └── books.sql
├── gen/                               # generated .NET project (created by `make gen`)
│   ├── Project.Data.csproj
│   ├── DbCtx.cs
│   ├── Extensions.cs
│   └── Query.cs
└── console/                           # sample app using the generated library
    ├── console.csproj
    └── Program.cs
```

```bash
cp env.example .env   # local DB settings
make gen              # build plugin + generate and build test/gen
make up-dep           # start PostgreSQL in Docker
make migrate          # apply migrations
make console          # run the sample app
make down-dep         # stop PostgreSQL when done
```

| Target           | Description                                       |
|------------------|---------------------------------------------------|
| `build`          | Build the plugin                                  |
| `gen`            | Build the plugin, generate code and build it      |
| `console`        | Run the sample console app                        |
| `up-dep`         | Start a PostgreSQL 17 container                   |
| `down-dep`       | Remove the PostgreSQL container                   |
| `migrate`        | Apply migrations                                  |
| `migrate-down`   | Roll back the last migration                      |
| `migrate-status` | Show migration status                             |

---

## Limitations

- PostgreSQL only.
- Only `:one`, `:many` and `:exec` queries are supported.
- Types not listed above (e.g. enums, `bytea`, `time`, `inet`) are not supported yet.
